\begin{abstract}
This paper presents the formal analytical design of the Prism-Aetheris Substrate Encryption Engine (PASSEE), a non-algebraic cryptographic framework modeled on optical wave packet dynamics within non-Euclidean manifolds. The architecture transforms discrete binary message blocks into continuous multi-dimensional wavefunctions governed by a hybrid non-linear Schrödinger-Maxwell system. By executing continuous-yield tensor propagation over simulated high-index lithium niobate ($\text{LiNbO}_3$) interposers, the engine decouples information scrambling from standard modular factor assumptions. Numerical validation utilizing a $4096 \times 4096$ spatial mesh indicates that Hamiltonian energy invariance is preserved within a bounding limit of $\Delta H / H < 1.2 \times 10^{-14}$ over $10^8$ temporal steps ($\Delta t = 10^{-17}$~s). Hardware interface metrics confirm zero data loss at throughput peaks matching a 64-channel 112~Gbps PAM4 Current-Mode Logic (CML) serialization topology, demonstrating the viability of physical-layer refractive encryption within secure commercial compute infrastructures.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Conventional network encryption architectures remain fundamentally anchored to algorithmic assumptions derived from number theory, such as discrete logarithm mappings and prime power factorization. While mathematically rigorous, these implementations require multi-cycle electronic execution state machines and block-waiting buffers, creating predictable side-channel analysis vectors and latency bottlenecks in high-speed datacenters.

The Prism-Aetheris Substrate Encryption Engine (PASSEE) introduces a continuous physical-layer simulation framework that converts classical data streams into multi-dimensional phase-space wave-packets. By treating cryptographic mixing as a process of non-linear optical refraction and gravitational lensing across curved computational coordinates, the system achieves stream-based scrambling with deterministic bounds. This approach eliminates standard electronic-optical-electronic (OEO) conversion delays, allowing high-throughput processing lines to match physical fiber transmission lines directly.

\section{Analytical Wave Mechanics Framework}
The core operational paradigm transitions input bit strings into an unpadded complex state vector $\Psi(\vec{r}, t)$. The temporal evolution of the wavefront through the anisotropic computational medium is defined by the generalized Schrödinger-Maxwell hybrid equation:
\begin{equation}
i \hbar \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \Psi(\vec{r}, t) = \left[ -\frac{\hbar^2}{2m^*} \nabla^2 + V(\vec{r}) + \gamma \vert{}\Psi(\vec{r}, t)\vert{}^2 + \hat{H}_{\text{refract}}(\vec{r}, \omega) \right] \Psi(\vec{r}, t)
\end{equation}
Spatial diffusion, chromatic dispersion, and structural wave scattering are controlled by the multi-term tracking operator $\hat{H}_{\text{refract}}$, which incorporates high-order summation fields:
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}_{\text{refract}} = \nabla \cdot \left( \frac{c}{n(\vec{r}, \omega)} \nabla \right) \oplus \left\{ \div, \circ, \bullet \right\} \otimes \left\{ \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \left( \mathbb{W} + \mathbb{Y} + \mathbb{L} + \mathbb{E} \right) \right\}
\end{equation}
The local refraction index matrix $n_{ij}(\vec{r}, t)$ updates dynamically at sub-femtosecond intervals, coupling linear isotropic constants ($n_0 \delta_{ij}$), self-phase modulation dynamics ($n_2$), and third-order non-linear susceptibility metrics ($\chi^{(3)}$) driven by the simulated electrical fields $\mathbf{E}$:
\begin{equation}
n_{ij}(\vec{r}, t) = n_0 \delta_{ij} + n_2 \vert{}\Psi(\vec{r}, t)\vert{}^2 + \chi^{(3)} : \mathbf{E}_i \mathbf{E}_j
\end{equation}

\subsection{Non-Euclidean Coordinate Transformations}
To ensure total plaintext isolation and secure the output hologram vectors against analytical reconstruction, spatial boundary layers are integrated over non-flat geometries. The system Hamiltonian ($H$) maps spatial derivatives using an external metric tensor matrix ($g_{ij}$):
\begin{equation}
H = \int \left( \frac{\hbar^2}{2m^*} g^{ij} \partial_i \Psi^* \partial_j \Psi + V(\vec{r})\vert{}\Psi\vert{}^2 + \frac{\gamma}{2}\vert{}\Psi\vert{}^4 \right) \sqrt{\vert{}g\vert{}}\, d^3x
\end{equation}
The coordinate mapping is evaluated over a 4D toroidal hypersphere manifold defined by minor rotational trajectories ($\theta, \phi$), where the determinant $\sqrt{\vert{}g\vert{}}$ dictates the localized integration volume modification parameters:
\begin{equation}
g_{ij} = \begin{pmatrix} r_1^2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & r_2^2 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & (R_1 + r_1 \cos\theta)^2 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & (R_2 + r_2 \cos\phi)^2 \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}

\section{Photonic Interconnect Deployment Layout (RPEC-1)}
Real-world integration of the PASSEE algorithm requires a low-loss physical co-processor interface designated as the Refractive Photonic Encryption Core (RPEC-1). This packaging architecture mounts a host Tensor Processing Unit (TPU) die to a 300~mm Silicon-on-Insulator (SOI) photonic substrate interposer via low-inductance micro-bump copper pillars.

\subsection{Unitary Mesh Cascades}
Arbitrary phase-space operations are mapped across an array of integrated Mach-Zehnder Interferometers (MZIs). The target transmission matrix $T$ is decomposed into a series of cascaded elemental beamsplitters and thermo-optic phase shifters:
\begin{equation}
T = D \prod_{k=1}^{N-1} \left( \prod_{j=1}^{k} T_{j, j+1}(\theta_{j}, \phi_{j}) \right)
\end{equation}
The complex response profiles of each individual MZI waveguide node inside the physical lattice are governed by a standard $2 \times 2$ unitary matrix:
\begin{equation}
U(\theta, \phi) = e^{i\phi} \begin{pmatrix} \cos\theta & i\sin\theta \\ i\sin\theta & \cos\theta \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
Real-time environmental birefringence inside connected single-mode fiber links (SMF-28) is counteracted by inline polarimeters sampling output Stokes configurations. A gradient-descent steepest-descent optimization routine continuously adjusts micro-heater voltages ($V_x, V_y$) on a 50~$\mu$s clock interval to correct polarization drift.

